<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1494">1494</a>–<a href="/years/1573">1573</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan Ginés de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sepulveda">Sepúlveda</a> was a Spanish humanist, philosopher and royal chronicler. Drawing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s idea of natural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, he argued that the Spanish conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> was just, because the indigenous peoples were barbarous and could be subjected for their own good. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid-debate">Valladolid debate</a> of 1550–1551 he defended this view against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a>, but the crown did not allow his treatise Democrates alter to be printed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-gines-de-sepulveda.jpg" alt="Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Maea · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_Gin%C3%A9s_de_Sep%C3%BAlveda_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sepulveda">Sepúlveda</a> was born at Pozoblanco in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, of humble background; his parents were Ginés Sánchez Mellado, a craftsman, and María Ruiz. In 1510 he enrolled at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-alcala">University of Alcalá</a> de Henares, where he studied philosophy and Greek, and in 1513 he began theology at the College of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-antonio">San Antonio</a> de Sigüenza. In February 1515 he obtained a letter of recommendation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-jimenez-de-cisneros">Cardinal Cisneros</a> to the Spanish College in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, where he matriculated that September. There he studied under Pietro Pomponazzi and gained a lifelong expertise in the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, encouraged by his patron Alberto Pío, Prince of Carpi, and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii">Giulio de' Medici</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>By 1523 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sepulveda">Sepúlveda</a> had earned doctorates in arts and theology and had taken religious vows as a Dominican priest. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii">Giulio de' Medici</a> became Pope Clement VII in that year, he served at the papal curia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where he gained a reputation for scholarship and rhetorical skill. He translated several works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> from Greek into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, among them the Parva Naturalia in 1522 and De mundo in 1523.</p><p>He also wrote on the religious controversies of the day: De fato et libero arbitrio against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> in 1526, De ritu nuptiarum in 1531 against the divorce proceedings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>, and the Antapologia against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> in 1532. He despised Luther, but respected Erasmus and corresponded with him for many years. In 1529 he wrote Ad Carolum V, urging Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> to unite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and fight the Turks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In Democrates, of 1535, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sepulveda">Sepúlveda</a> defended the idea that a just war based on natural law was compatible with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. His case for the conquest drew also on Saint Thomas Aquinas, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> of just war and the right of conquest, and on the Bible, which he used to justify forced conversion; his position differed from that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/school-of-salamanca">School of Salamanca</a> represented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-vitoria">Francisco de Vitoria</a>. He had never been to America. He claimed that some, though not all, Amerindians were natural slaves as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> defined them in Book I of the Politics, and compared them to children before parents.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a> used the same sources in reply. He held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> should be offered to the natives as an option, not imposed as an obligation, and that no Amerindians lacked the ability to rule themselves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sepulveda">Sepúlveda</a>'s later works include histories of the deeds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, of 1556, and of Philip II, of 1564, and De regno, of 1570. He died on 17 November 1573.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the University of Salamanca, Francisco de Vitoria and his successors developed ideas of natural law, the rights of peoples and economics that influenced international law, and the debate at Valladolid in 1550–1551 between Bartolomé de las Casas and <b>Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda</b> examined the justice of conquest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Casas's campaign against the abuse of indigenous peoples helped produce the New Laws of 1542, which sought, with mixed success, to curb the encomienda and outlaw the enslavement of indigenous Americans, and in the Valladolid debate of 1550–1551 he argued against <b>Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda</b>'s justification of conquest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…of the Destruction of the Indies (written 1542, printed 1552), the theologian Francisco de Vitoria questioned the grounds of Spanish dominion in his lectures of 1539, and at Valladolid in 1550–1551 Las Casas debated <b>Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda</b>, who defended the conquest with Aristotle’s theory of natural slavery.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Aristotle himself was cited in its defence: at the Valladolid debate of 1550–1551 <b>Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda</b> justified the Spanish conquest with Aristotle's theory of natural slavery, against Bartolomé de las Casas, who had denounced the destruction of the Caribbean peoples.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-should-rule">Who Should Rule?</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pozoblanco, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Pozoblanco, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.38,-4.85&amp;year=1545" aria-label="Pozoblanco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4429 785 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4879 1055h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4879 1055h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.38,-4.85&amp;year=1545">What was here in 1545? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
